<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

	<docinfo/>

	<slideshow>
		<title>Getting Started with <pretext/></title>
		<subtitle>Write, Preview, and Publish in Two Hours</subtitle>

		<xi:include href="./frontmatter-slides.ptx" />

		<slide label="welcome">
			<title>Welcome</title>

			<p>
				Thanks for being here. Over the next two hours, you will
				author, build, and publish a small lesson using <pretext/>
				entirely in your web browser.
			</p>

			<p pause="no">
				This workshop assumes you are:
			</p>

			<ul pause="no">
				<li>An instructor curious about authoring your own course materials</li>
				<li>New to <pretext/>.</li>
				<li>Comfortable editing text and navigating a browser</li>
			</ul>

		</slide>

        <slide label="the-promise">
            <title>What You Will Leave With</title>

            <p>
                By the end of this session, you will have:
            </p>

            <dl pause="no">
                <li>
                    <title>A working authoring setup</title>
                    <p>Running in your browser, ready to use again tomorrow.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>A small lesson you wrote yourself</title>
                    <p>Built from real <pretext/> source, on a topic you actually teach.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>A shareable link</title>
                    <p>A public URL you can send to a colleague or a student.</p>
                </li>
            </dl>

            <p>
                That's the whole goal. Everything else is a bonus.
            </p>
        </slide>

		<slide label="what-is-pretext">
			<title>What Is <pretext/>?</title>

			<p>
				<pretext/> is a free, open-source authoring system for
				textbooks, lecture notes, worksheets, and articles.
			</p>

			<p>
				The key idea is simple: you describe <em>what your content is</em>,
				such as a definition, an example, or an exercise, and <pretext/>
				decides how it should look.
			</p>

			<p>
				One source file. Many polished outputs.
			</p>
		</slide>

        <slide label="write-once">
            <title>Write Once, Publish Everywhere</title>

            <p pause="no">
                From a single <pretext/> source, you can generate:
            </p>

            <ul pause="no">
                <li>An interactive website</li>
                <li>A print-ready PDF</li>
                <li>An ePub for e-readers</li>
                <li>Accessible alternative formats when students need them</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                You write your content once. When tomorrow's format comes
                along, your source is ready for it.
            </p>
        </slide>

		<slide label="why-care">
			<title>Why Instructors Care</title>

			<dl pause="no">
				<li>
					<title>Accessibility by default</title>
					<p>Screen-reader friendly, keyboard navigable, and ready for students who need alternative formats, all without extra work from you.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Your content, your control</title>
					<p>Free, open, and yours to adapt, remix, and share.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Built for the long run</title>
					<p>Structured source that will still be usable in ten years.</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</slide>

		<slide label="latex-bridge">
			<title>If You Know <latex/>, You Have a Head Start</title>

			<p>
				Much of what you already know transfers directly.
			</p>

			<tabular>
				<row>
					<cell>Inline math</cell><cell><pre>\(*\)</pre></cell><cell><pre>&lt;m&gt;*&lt;/m&gt;</pre></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Displayed math</cell><cell><pre>\[*\]</pre></cell><cell><pre>&lt;md&gt;*&lt;/md&gt;</pre></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Ordered Lists</cell>
					<cell>
						<pre>
							\begin{enumerate}
								\item *
							\end{enumerate}
						</pre>
					</cell>
					<cell>
						<pre>
							&lt;ol&gt;
								&lt;li&gt;*&lt;/li&gt;
							&lt;/ol&gt;
						</pre>
					</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<p>
				The math inside the tags is still <latex/>. You are mostly
				learning a new wrapper, not a new language.
			</p>

		</slide>

        <slide label="today-plan">
            <title>Today's Plan</title>

            <dl pause="no">
                <li>
                    <title>First: everyone gets an account</title>
                    <p>A free PreTeXt.Plus account in a browser tab.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Warm-up: build a syllabus together</title>
                    <p>Four short parts. Paste, rebuild, transform.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Main event: build a worksheet lesson</title>
                    <p>A product-rule worksheet you can adapt for your own class.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Close: share, see each other's work, what's next</title>
                    <p>Your lesson goes live on a public URL.</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </slide>

        <slide label="how-we-work">
            <title>How We Will Work Together</title>

            <ul pause="no">
                <li>Follow along on your own laptop; this is hands-on, not a lecture</li>
                <li>Keep the workshop hub open in a second tab for the cheatsheet and timeline</li>
                <li>Ask your neighbor first; ask me second</li>
                <li>Ask questions out loud when it makes sense; someone else probably has the same one</li>
                <li>If something breaks, that's normal</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                The goal is <em>finished</em>, not <em>perfect</em>.
            </p>
        </slide>

		<slide label="ready">
			<title>Ready?</title>

			<p>
				Open your laptop, open a browser, and go to
				<c>pretext.plus</c>.
			</p>

			<p>
				In a moment we will all sign up together.
			</p>

			<p>
				Let's build something.
			</p>
		</slide>

	</slideshow>

</pretext>
